## 1. How GitHub Pages Works

Think of the process as three parts:

1. **Development** — You write your website using HTML, CSS, JavaScript, or a static site generator.
2. **Repository** — You upload your website source code to a GitHub repository.
3. **Deployment** — GitHub Pages builds and publishes the website to a public URL.

```
Your website files
index.html · style.css · script.js · assets/
        │
        ▼
    Git push
        │
        ▼
GitHub repository
Your source code and version history
        │
        ▼
GitHub Actions / Pages build
        │
        ▼
Published website
https://username.github.io/
```

GitHub Pages can publish from a repository branch **or** through a GitHub Actions workflow.

---

## 2. Types of GitHub Pages Websites

There are two primary types.

### A. User or Organization Website

This is your main personal or organization website.

| Item | Value |
|------|-------|
| **Repository name** | `username.github.io` |
| **Example** | `emon.github.io` |
| **Published URL** | `https://emon.github.io/` |

You can use this website for your portfolio, personal blog, or a central landing page for all your projects.

### B. Project Website

A project website belongs to an individual repository.

| Item | Value |
|------|-------|
| **Repository** | `QDoc` |
| **Username** | `emon` |
| **Published URL** | `https://emon.github.io/QDoc/` |

You can create a separate website for every repository that needs one.

### Comparison

| Feature | User Website | Project Website |
|---------|-------------|-----------------|
| Repository name | `username.github.io` | Any repository |
| URL | `/` | `/repository/` |
| Purpose | Personal website | Individual project |
| Number | One primary site per account | Multiple |

