## 3. Technologies Supported by GitHub Pages

GitHub Pages serves **static files**. You can use several technologies to create those files.

| Technology | Supported | Purpose |
|------------|-----------|---------|
| HTML | Yes | Website structure |
| CSS | Yes | Styling and layouts |
| JavaScript | Yes | Interactivity |
| TypeScript | After compilation | Application logic |
| Markdown | Yes | Articles and documentation |
| JSON | Yes | Static data |
| SVG | Yes | Vector graphics |
| React | After building | Component-based UI |
| Vue | After building | Frontend applications |
| Angular | After building | Frontend applications |
| Vite | After building | Frontend development |
| Jekyll | Native support | Static site generation |
| PHP | No server execution | Backend |
| Node.js | No server execution | Backend |
| Python | No server execution | Backend |
| MySQL | No | Database server |

&gt; **Important distinction:** You can develop a React application, but GitHub Pages cannot execute your React source code directly. You must compile it into HTML, CSS and JavaScript first.
&gt;
&gt; Similarly, you can use JavaScript to communicate with external APIs, but GitHub Pages itself does not provide a backend server.

