## 4. Creating Your First GitHub Pages Website

Let's create a website from scratch using plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript.

### Step 1 — Create a GitHub Repository

1. Open GitHub → **Create a new repository**.
2. Enter your repository name.
3. Choose **Public** if you're using the free plan.
4. Optionally select **Add a README file**.
5. Click **Create repository**.

For a personal website, name it:

```
yourusername.github.io
```

For a project website, you can use any repository name, such as:

```
my-website
```

### Step 2 — Create Your Website Files

A simple project structure:

```
my-website/
├── index.html      ← Entry point
├── style.css
├── script.js
└── assets/
    └── images/
```

&gt; GitHub Pages recognizes `index.html`, `index.md` or `README.md` as an entry file. For a conventional website, use `index.html`.

### Step 3 — Write Your Website

**Create `index.html`:**

```html



  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>My GitHub Website</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">


  <header>
    <h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>
    <p>Built and hosted with GitHub Pages.</p>
  </header>

  <main>
    <h2>About Me</h2>
    <p>This is my personal website.</p>
    <button id="hello">Say Hello</button>
    <p id="message"></p>
  </main>

  


```

**Create `style.css`:**

```css
body {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
  max-width: 900px;
  margin: 60px auto;
  padding: 20px;
  line-height: 1.7;
  color: #263238;
  background: #f8fafc;
}

header {
  padding: 40px 0;
  border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;
}

button {
  padding: 10px 20px;
  border: 0;
  background: #0969da;
  color: white;
  cursor: pointer;
}
```

**Create `script.js`:**

```javascript
document.getElementById("hello").addEventListener("click", () =&gt; {
  document.getElementById("message").textContent =
    "Hello! Welcome to my GitHub Pages website.";
});
```

### Step 4 — Upload Your Files

You have two options:

**Option A: GitHub Website**

1. Open your repository.
2. Click **Add file** → **Upload files**.
3. Upload your HTML, CSS and JavaScript files.
4. Click **Commit changes**.

**Option B: Git on Ubuntu**

Since you use Ubuntu, you can manage your website directly from the terminal:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git

cd REPOSITORY

# Add or edit your website files

git add .
git commit -m "Create website"
git push origin main
```

### Step 5 — Enable GitHub Pages

Open your repository:

1. Go to **Settings**.
2. Select **Pages** from the sidebar.
3. Find **Build and deployment**.
4. Under **Source**, select **Deploy from a branch**.
5. Select **main**.
6. Select **/(root)**.
7. Click **Save**.

After deployment, your website should be available at:

```
https://USERNAME.github.io/REPOSITORY/
```

For a personal website:

```
https://USERNAME.github.io/
```

&gt; The first deployment can take several minutes. You can find the published URL and deployment status in **Settings → Pages**.

