## 8. Jekyll and Static Site Generators

GitHub Pages has **native integration with Jekyll**.

Jekyll converts Markdown, templates, layouts and other source files into a complete static website.

For example:

```
my-website/
│
├── _config.yml
├── _layouts/
│   └── default.html
├── _includes/
│   └── navigation.html
├── _posts/
│   └── 2026-11-23-first-post.md
├── assets/
│   └── style.css
└── index.md
```

### What Does Jekyll Provide?

| Feature | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| Layouts | Reuse common page structures |
| Includes | Reuse navigation, footer and components |
| Collections | Organize documentation and content |
| Posts | Create blog articles |
| Front matter | Store page metadata |
| Liquid | Dynamic template rendering |
| Plugins | Extend the build system |
| Themes | Apply predefined website designs |

For example, a Markdown blog article:

```markdown
---
title: My First Article
date: 2026-11-23
---

# Welcome

This is my first blog post.
```

Jekyll can convert it into an HTML page automatically.

### Other Static Site Generators

You aren't restricted to Jekyll.

| Tool | Best For |
|------|----------|
| **Vite** | Vanilla JavaScript, React and Vue applications. Generates production files, usually in `dist/`. |
| **Astro** | Documentation, blogs, content-focused websites and fast landing pages. |
| **Docusaurus** | Software documentation, versioned documentation, sidebars and search integrations. |
| **Hugo** | Fast static site generation for blogs, documentation and content-heavy websites. |

&gt; For these tools, use **GitHub Actions** to build the website and deploy its output.

