## 9. Repository Features Related to Website Development

GitHub offers several features that make website development easier.

| GitHub Feature | What It Does |
|----------------|-------------|
| Repositories | Store your website source code |
| Branches | Maintain separate development versions |
| Commits | Track individual changes |
| Pull requests | Review proposed changes |
| Issues | Track bugs and improvements |
| Discussions | Community communication |
| Actions | Automate builds and deployments |
| Releases | Publish versioned software |
| Projects | Organize development tasks |
| Environments | Control deployment environments |
| Secrets | Store sensitive workflow credentials |
| Webhooks | Notify external services of events |
| Dependabot | Help identify dependency updates |

### Branches and Website Development

You don't have to make every change directly on `main`.

For example:

```
main
 │
 ├── feature/new-homepage
 │
 ├── feature/dark-mode
 │
 └── fix/navigation
```

You can develop a feature on a separate branch, test it, and then merge it into `main`.

If your Pages source is `main`, merging into `main` triggers a new deployment.

### Pull Requests

A typical workflow:

1. Create a feature branch.
2. Make changes.
3. Push the branch.
4. Open a pull request.
5. Review the changes.
6. Merge into `main`.
7. GitHub Pages deploys the updated website.

This is particularly useful when working with other developers.

