## 15. How to Monitor and Manage Deployments

GitHub provides a dedicated interface for monitoring your website.

Open your repository and select the **Actions** tab.

You can inspect:

- Whether a deployment succeeded or failed.
- Which commit triggered the deployment.
- Which workflow executed.
- Build and deployment logs.
- Failed steps and error messages.
- Previous deployment runs.
- Manually rerun failed workflows.

You can also visit:

```
Repository → Settings → Pages
```

This displays your publishing source, deployment information and website URL.

---

## 16. Recommended Website Development Workflow

For a professional development process, I recommend **separating your source code from your deployment process**.

```
Development Environment
Ubuntu desktop
VS Code / Sublime Text / Terminal
        │
        ▼
Source Code
website/
├── src/
├── public/
├── package.json
├── index.html
└── vite.config.js
        │
        ▼
Version Control
GitHub repository
Branches, commits and pull requests
        │
        ▼
Automated Deployment
GitHub Actions
Build and deploy on every push
        │
        ▼
Production
GitHub Pages
Live website with HTTPS
```

**My suggested approach:**

1. Write your website locally on Ubuntu.
2. Use vanilla JavaScript or Vite, depending on project complexity.
3. Store the source code in GitHub.
4. Use GitHub Actions for automated deployment.
5. Use a custom domain if you want a professional address.
6. Keep your development and production environments separate.

This workflow is scalable enough for personal websites, documentation and many frontend applications.

