## 18. Final Understanding

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| Is GitHub Pages free? | Yes, subject to plan and usage limits |
| Do I need a domain? | No |
| Can I use my own domain? | Yes |
| Can I update my website anytime? | Yes |
| Does it automatically deploy? | Yes, when configured |
| Can I host multiple websites? | Yes |
| Can I use vanilla JavaScript? | Yes |
| Can I use React and Vite? | Yes, after building |
| Can I host a backend? | No |
| Can I host documentation? | Yes |
| Does it provide HTTPS? | Yes |
| Can I use GitHub Actions? | Yes |

&gt; **The central idea:** GitHub is where you store and manage your website's source code. GitHub Actions can build it, and GitHub Pages hosts the resulting static website.

### What I Would Recommend Learning Next

Since you're interested in vanilla JavaScript and building software projects, a particularly useful next step would be learning **how to deploy a complete vanilla JavaScript application using GitHub Pages**, including:

- Multiple HTML pages
- Routing
- Assets
- JSON data
- Automated deployment

That knowledge will let you publish actual software projects rather than just simple static webpages.

